-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The server assigns:
+- Submission timestamp
+- Revision ID (unique within the visit; incremented on each new submission)
+- A stored reference to the submission's payload for audit trail
+
+## Current-State Representation
+
+A visit's current state is a single selected revision. The response to a retrieval includes:
+
+- The current revision ID
+- The current data (the submitted payload from that revision)
+- A flag indicating whether unresolved competing revisions exist
+- If unresolved: a list of competing revision IDs and their brief summaries (author, submission time, base revision referenced)
+
+If no revisions exist (visit not yet submitted), the response indicates that.
+
+## Unresolved-Work Representation
+
+When competing revisions exist, each alternate revision is available for review:
+
+- Revision ID and author
+- Base revision ID (what it was derived from)
+- Submission timestamp
+- Full submitted data
+- A summary of how it differs from the current selected revision (field-by-field diff summary for coordinator context)
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator supplies:
+
+- Visit ID and target revision ID to select
+- Resolution notes (free-form explanation of the decision)
+
+The store records the resolution as:
+- The coordinator's identity
+- The list of contributing revision IDs involved in the choice
+- The resolution notes
+- Resolution timestamp
+
+The selected revision becomes the current state. Rejected revisions remain stored and remain visible in the unresolved-work list until the coordinator explicitly selects one.
+
+## Tablet-Sync Contract
+
+Tablet-sync retains the base revision ID for each locally edited visit. On submission success, the store returns the assigned revision ID. Tablet-sync displays competing revisions in the pending-review state, referencing this node's unresolved-work representation. Field-sync continues when a coordinator resolves (moving the visit to the newly selected revision state).
+
+## Review-Export Contract
+
+Review-export queries the store for all visits. The store distinguishes:
+
+- Ready for export: current state selected, no unresolved competing revisions
+- Pending coordinator review: competing revisions exist
+
+Review-export excludes pending-review visits from its CSV export until a coordinator resolves them. Review-export retrieves the full unresolved-work list for visits in pending state, presenting a revision comparison view to coordinators. The resolution operation records which revision was chosen and the coordinator's notes.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |
+|---|---|---|
+| Whole-visit revisions with manual coordinator selection | Preserves each submitted visit as a unit; avoids maintaining merge rules that could miss scientific relationships. Trade: every concurrent edit, even to unrelated fields, requires review. | Field-aware three-way merge: automatically combines disjoint field edits, reducing review volume; requires maintained merge rules and risks combining edits across unrecognized scientific relationships. |
+| Revision ID assigned by server, not client | Ensures uniqueness and prevents clients from guessing unresolved revision states. Requires store to be authoritative. | Client-assigned revision IDs: reduces server load but risks collision and requires more complex conflict detection. |
+| Base revision ID in submission envelope | Tablets can track which state an edit was derived from; server can detect concurrent branches from the same ancestor. Enables clear three-way presentation to coordinators. | Submission carries full base snapshot instead: larger payload; less clear to coordinators which exact revision was the ancestor. |
+| Resolution selects a single revision, not synthetic merge | Preserves one worker's complete submitted data rather than mixing submissions. Aligns with the tenet "do not silently discard." | Synthetic merge: coordinator manually constructs a combined visit data; adds complexity, loses attribution of which fields came from which submission. |
